56	\subsection{Router Training as Diagnostic Probe}
57	\label{sec:router-diagnostic}
58	
59	We probe whether counterfactual labels can train a step-level router, as a diagnostic of label informativeness. An implicit router (Qwen3-1.7B fine-tuned; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}) yields $54.1 \pm 5.4\%$ step-level balanced accuracy but $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$ on held-out episodes (8 seeds), below always-deterministic (50\%). A controlled replication using identical 4-seed configurations for all three scales yields 0.6B: $50.7\%$, 1.7B: $43.1 \pm 1.0\%$, 4B: $59.0\%$, confirming non-monotonic (U-shaped) capacity scaling. The 1.7B dip is robust across seeds (range $41.7$--$44.4\%$), ruling out the prior configuration confound. This non-monotonic pattern, combined with universally low internal recall, reinforces that learned routing does not scale predictably with capacity at current data scales. Only 4B exceeds the baseline ($58.96 \pm 1.94\%$, $p < 0.005$; Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}). Augmentation-based data scaling ($25\%$--$800\%$) provides no improvement (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}), but paraphrase augmentation inflates the majority class (89.6\%$\rightarrow$97.7\%) without generating nov
